From 5f866de60329e43af555752b912cfbb32f5dc28d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Somalapuram Amaranath <somalapuram@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 09:15:53 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] zink: apply the dma-buf plane offset when binding a
 single-plane import

A non-DISJOINT dma-buf import can start partway into the shared buffer.
That is the case for every non-first plane of a YUV buffer that the GL
frontend has lowered into separate single-plane R8 images: dri2 imports
each plane as its own resource whose data begins at whandle->offset.

zink only captured that offset on the modifier (is_aux) and DISJOINT
paths. The plain single-plane path bound the VkImage at obj->offset,
which is 0 for a dedicated import, so every chroma plane bound at the
start of the buffer and sampled the LUMA plane as chroma.

Observed on an RTX 3070 Ti (zink on NVK) playing a 1920x1080 YV12 video:
the picture came out green and magenta while all RGB content was
correct. Solving the YUV->RGB matrix backwards from the recorded frame
gives U == V in every region -- the fingerprint of two planes reading the
same memory. Native GL drivers (radeonsi, iris) import planes themselves
and never hit this, which is why the same image played video correctly
on the AMD and Intel machines.

Add the whandle offset at the non-DISJOINT bind. Non-import allocations
have no whandle, and the luma plane's offset is 0, so nothing else
changes.

Change-Id: Ieaeca197b633a6b2c55b2ce8c8b481e186aaa0aa
---
 src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_resource.c | 20 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_resource.c b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_resource.c
index 834bfeac433..49f40ac90cc 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_resource.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_resource.c
@@ -1479,8 +1479,26 @@ create_image(struct zink_screen *screen, struct zink_resource_object *obj,
          return roc_fail_and_cleanup_all;
       }
    } else {
+      /* A non-DISJOINT dma-buf import can still start partway into the shared
+       * buffer. That happens for every non-first plane of a YUV buffer that the
+       * GL frontend lowered into separate single-plane R8 images: each plane is
+       * imported as its own image whose data begins at whandle->offset. Only the
+       * modifier (is_aux) and DISJOINT paths applied that offset; this plain
+       * single-plane path bound at obj->offset alone, which for a fresh import is
+       * 0. So both chroma planes bound at the start of the buffer and sampled the
+       * LUMA plane as chroma. Measured on the RTX 3070 Ti (zink on NVK): decoded
+       * video came out green/magenta with U == V everywhere -- the signature of
+       * two planes reading the same memory -- while RGB was correct. Native GL
+       * drivers (radeonsi/iris) import planes themselves and never hit this, so
+       * it was NVIDIA-only. Add the plane offset here. Non-import allocations
+       * have no whandle (offset stays obj->offset) and the luma plane's offset is
+       * 0, so nothing else changes.
+       */
+      VkDeviceSize bind_offset = obj->offset;
+      if (alloc_info->whandle)
+         bind_offset += alloc_info->whandle->offset;
       if (!(templ->flags & PIPE_RESOURCE_FLAG_SPARSE))
-         if (VKSCR(BindImageMemory)(screen->dev, obj->image, zink_bo_get_mem(obj->bo), obj->offset) != VK_SUCCESS) {
+         if (VKSCR(BindImageMemory)(screen->dev, obj->image, zink_bo_get_mem(obj->bo), bind_offset) != VK_SUCCESS) {
             mesa_loge("ZINK: vkBindImageMemory failed");
             return roc_fail_and_cleanup_all;
          }
